# Line endings, declared rather than left to whoever cloned this.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. Without it, what lands in a working copy depends on the
# machine's own core.autocrlf, so two clones of one commit can differ - and the
# consumer here is RAD Studio, which writes CRLF for everything it touches. A
# tool that edits a source with LF then leaves the IDE re-saving it with CRLF,
# and the next diff is the whole file. The same rule is declared in the PasTree
# repo (object-pascal-tree/.gitattributes); keep the two in step.
#
# Everything is normalized to LF IN THE REPOSITORY; eol= controls what the
# working copy gets. Delphi sources and anything cmd.exe parses get CRLF.

* text=auto

# Delphi sources and projects - CRLF, the way the IDE writes them.
*.pas   text eol=crlf
*.dpr   text eol=crlf
*.dpk   text eol=crlf
*.inc   text eol=crlf
*.dproj text eol=crlf
*.dfm   text eol=crlf
*.groupproj text eol=crlf

# Batch files: cmd.exe is the one interpreter here that can genuinely misparse
# an LF-only file, and every build in this repo goes through one.
*.bat   text eol=crlf

# PowerShell, called from those batch files for the few things cmd cannot do.
# CRLF for the same reason and one of its own: a here-string or a line
# continuation in a script Windows PowerShell reads as LF-only is a class of
# parse error that reads as a logic bug.
*.ps1   text eol=crlf

# Resource scripts, compiled by brcc32 - a Windows tool, like the rest.
*.rc    text eol=crlf

# Documentation and data - LF, which is what their own tooling and every
# diff viewer expect. `.calls` are the CLI scripts of testssmoke.ps1.
*.md    text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.calls text eol=lf

# Shell scripts, and the hooks that ARE shell scripts without an extension -
# LF, or /bin/sh chokes on the CR: `#!/bin/sh\r` is not an interpreter. The
# irony is deliberate to record: without these two lines the `* text=auto`
# above hands a CRLF working copy to the very hook that exists to keep CRLF
# where it belongs, and it stops running.
*.sh          text eol=lf
.githooks/*   text eol=lf

# Git's own configuration, LF like the rest of the tooling - and not left to
# `text=auto`, which is how this file came to be the one exception to the rule
# it declares.
.gitattributes text eol=lf
.gitignore     text eol=lf
LICENSE text eol=lf

# Compiled/binary output that may end up tracked: never touched.
*.res   binary
*.ico   binary
